{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2. Seven pm.",
  "Phil said in the lobby: Rita. I hear you. About Alma. I'll call her from the terminal tomorrow, after I'm off the coach. I'll ask her what she wants to hear. I'll just... listen. I'll do it from a payphone with a three-minute limit and I'll do it. Goodnight.",
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Larry said in the lobby: I'm taking the last charge off the festival board. Rialto, before it closes. Don't wait on me for the afternoon.",
  "Larry records Alma on his last charged take. The reel stays with him.",
  "You are now at Rialto.",
  "There is time for one evening visit. The ballroom lights are on and the station line is open."
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "what_you_did": "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
   "what_you_said": "I'm taking the afternoon. Rialto closes at six. I want to be in that booth before the lights go out for good. Don't wait up.",
   "your_intention": "Get to the Rialto before nine. Sit in the booth where Alma spent forty-two winters threading film. Record her one last time—her hands on the gate, her voice saying what she wants her daughter to know. One reel. The regional line takes it out tonight. Pittsburgh gets the voice. That is the piece. Not the party. Not the ballroom. Not the network's story. Mine."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "your present location",
 "physical_choices": {
  "transmit_interview": "Take the regional line at the station and send any usable interview with Alma to her daughter.",
  "go_dancing": "Spend the evening at the ballroom, open to dancing with a colleague.",
  "go_to_party_for_work": "Cover the party with your notebook, without dancing.",
  "take_evening_off": "Choose a quiet evening on your own."
 }
}

{
  "action": "transmit_interview",
  "detail": "Rita picks up the receiver on the station line. The dial tone is already there, warm and waiting. She slots the reel into the patch bay—Alma's hands on the gate, Alma's voice saying what she wants her daughter to hear—and presses the connect. The line hums. The reel turns. She does not watch it. She stands with her weight on the back foot, one hand still on the receiver, the other flat on the cold metal of the projection booth, the way you rest a palm on a church pew when the service is over and you can't decide to leave. Three minutes. Maybe four. The reel ends. The spool slows. She sets the receiver down. The cradle clicks. She does not pick it up again. She turns off the overhead fluorescent. In the dark, the booth smells like hot film and dust and forty-two winters of someone else's Tuesday evenings. She stays. Not because there is anything left to do. Because the chair is still warm where she was sitting, and the gate is still closed, and that is enough. She will walk home. She will not go to the ballroom. She will not write a note to Phil about the payphone. She will not call Pittsburgh to confirm the line held. It held. She heard the hum change when the reel hit the end. That is the confirmation. She pulls her coat on. She does not lock the booth. She leaves it the way Alma left it every night for forty-two winters: gate closed, spool seated, light off. The piece is out. The voice is going. She is done.",
  "intention": "Get Alma's voice to her daughter in Pittsburgh tonight, on one reel, on the regional line. Then be still. Then go home. Not to the ballroom. Not to the network's story. The piece is the voice. The piece is done. Sit in the dark for a moment where the hands were, and then walk.",
  "words": "None. She does not say anything to the receiver. She does not say anything to the booth. The reel talks. That is enough."
}
